# House workflow

This project follows the **prompt-utils** methodology. The standards are checked out at `.standards/` and are **authoritative** — where they and your instincts disagree, they win.

Read the relevant document before writing code. Do not work from memory of what these rules "usually" say.

## Where the standards are

```
.standards/WORKFLOW.md                     the methodology overview — read this first
.standards/workflow/stage-1-viability/     is the idea worth building (GO / NO-GO / PIVOT)
.standards/workflow/stage-2-mockup/        the complete visual surface, against a mock layer
.standards/workflow/stage-3-openapi/       the OpenAPI contract + backend behavioural brief
.standards/workflow/stage-4-backend/       backend implementation
  ├── backend/                               20 layer folders (01-dependencies … 20-build-packaging)
  ├── sql/                                   database schema and migrations
  └── tests/                                 the six-path testing strategy
.standards/workflow/stage-5-frontend/      non-visual frontend architecture (26 layer folders)
.standards/workflow/stage-6-integration/   wiring frontend to a live backend
.standards/workflow/stage-7-deployment/    IaC provisioning and deployment
.standards/workflow/stage-8-polish/        small/medium changes to a shipped app
.standards/workflow/stage-10-feature/      large features on an isolated branch
```

Every stage folder holds the same three things:

- **`PROMPT.md`** — how to execute the stage.
- **`checklist.md`** — the quality gate. **This is what the PR reviewer checks against**, so treat it as the definition of done.
- **Numbered sub-folders** — one per layer/concern, each with a `CREATE-prompt.md`.

If `.standards/` is missing, stop and ask for it to be cloned (see `.cursor/rules/README.md`). Do not guess at the standards.

## Which stage owns what

| You are changing | Stage that owns it | Read |
|---|---|---|
| Components, pages, design tokens, styles, animations, copy, mock handlers | **Stage 2** | `.standards/workflow/stage-2-mockup/checklist.md` |
| The API contract (`openapi.json`, error envelope, pagination) | **Stage 3** | `.standards/workflow/stage-3-openapi/checklist.md` |
| Entities, repositories, services, use cases, controllers, DTOs, jobs | **Stage 4** | `.standards/workflow/stage-4-backend/backend/checklist.md` |
| Schema and migrations | **Stage 4 / sql** | `.standards/workflow/stage-4-backend/sql/checklist.md` |
| State, guards, hooks, real auth, analytics, storage, error reporting | **Stage 5** | `.standards/workflow/stage-5-frontend/checklist.md` |
| Connecting the frontend to a live backend, e2e smoke flows | **Stage 6** | `.standards/workflow/stage-6-integration/` |
| Infrastructure, secrets, releases | **Stage 7** | `.standards/workflow/stage-7-deployment/checklist.md` |

Picking the wrong stage is the most common way to produce work that fails review — the stages have explicit ownership boundaries and crossing one is a violation regardless of how good the code is.

## Non-negotiables

1. **Never re-author another stage's territory.** Each stage's `PROMPT.md` carries an anti-rewrite contract naming exactly what it may not touch. If the change you need lives in another stage's territory, that is a route-back, not a patch.
2. **Never invent backend surface.** The frontend calls only operations that exist in `openapi.json`. A missing operation is a route-back to Stage 3/4, not an assumption.
3. **Route-backs are recorded, not improvised.** When a stage is blocked by an upstream gap, write an RB ticket in `route-backs.md` with an explicit **A** (resolve upstream) or **B** (defer behind an off-by-default flag) decision. Do not silently downgrade a capability.
4. **The checklist is the definition of done.** Before saying a task is complete, walk the relevant `checklist.md` and confirm each applicable item. Items marked N/A need a written justification.

## Repository layout

Product code lives under `apps/`:

```
apps/<name>-frontend/    frontend application
apps/<name>-backend/     backend service
```

The PR pipeline detects which area a change belongs to from both the commit header and these paths, and applies that area's standards. Putting code in the wrong place changes which rules it is judged by.
